{"description":"Follow-up finding from CTX-0294 live wiring. State::resize reflows width and height in one pass; blank rows below the content are collected as physical rows, so shrinking height by one in the same call as a width change moves the top content row into scrollback while the grid keeps the blank tail (observed: 80x24 "hello world" -> resize(38,23) yields sb=1 and a blank grid). The runtime works around it with a two-phase resize (width at old height, then height-only) in Runtime::resize_primary_grid and pane sync, but the underlying State::resize semantics remain surprising and are untested for this shape.

Deliverable: decide and test the intended one-call semantic (keep cursor-anchored bottom-align for real content but not drop top content behind trailing blanks), and remove the runtime workaround once State::resize preserves the visible content.

Priority: P2 | Area: term-state | Labels: fix,area:term-state,P2 | Milestone: v0.1.0 | Task: CTX-0294 (follow-up)","displayId":"CTX-0312","id":"01M265DMBVPF231TRM0WPXJ9SD","priority":"normal","status":"ready","title":"term-state: one-call width+height shrink bottom-aligns top content into scrollback"}
